2186361655 13194139535694 true] [17592186124457 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361676 13194139535694 true] [17592186124457 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361711 13194139535694 true] [17592186124458 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361683 13194139535694 true] [17592186124458 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361697 13194139535694 true] [17592186124458 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361704 13194139535694 true] [17592186124459 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361662 13194139535694 true] [17592186124459 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361669 13194139535694 true] [17592186124473 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361648 13194139535694 true] [17592186124473 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361690 13194139535694 true] [17592186125022 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535694 true] [17592186125022 1100 "Given the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x) = x^{22} - x^{20} + 3x - 2$, find $f(A)$." 13194139535694 false] [17592186125022 1100 "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$" 13194139535694 true] [17592186125022 1145 "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+8 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-1\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{22}\\,-\\,{x}^{20}\\,+\\,3x\\,-\\,2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{22}$ | $-$ | ${x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $3x$ | $-$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(3x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{20}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =3A-2I \\\\  & =3[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535694 true] [17592186125055 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535694 true] [17592186125055 1100 "Given that $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$." 13194139535694 false] [17592186125055 1100 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" 13194139535694 true] [17592186125055 1145 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=-12+3=-9.\n$$" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361648 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361649 13194139535694 true] [17592186361648 1089 "q-140519" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361648 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535694 true] [17592186361648 1100 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361648 1113 #uuid "c9130178-79b6-5431-9c03-0a59d7f3d7bd" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361648 1145 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=4+1=5.\n$$" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361650 13194139535694 true] [17592186361649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361651 13194139535694 true] [17592186361649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361652 13194139535694 true] [17592186361649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361653 13194139535694 true] [17592186361649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361654 13194139535694 true] [17592186361649 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535694 true] [17592186361649 1041 "selection" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361649 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361654 13194139535694 true] [17592186361649 1110 #uuid "813f3453-52e4-5a3f-9bdd-5f75f0a28817" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361650 1034 "10" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361650 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535694 true] [17592186361650 1111 #uuid "68fb280e-41bd-58de-b8fb-7a94029b73b8" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361651 1034 "8" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361651 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535694 true] [17592186361651 1111 #uuid "8746d15a-9c6d-561f-a33b-c1fd942129a0" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361652 1034 "6" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361652 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535694 true] [17592186361652 1111 #uuid "2dfff2b4-7787-59d5-96da-1b6fddf6b7d7" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361653 1034 "4" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361653 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535694 true] [17592186361653 1111 #uuid "07eb84d4-f94a-5668-992e-4902a708b4fd" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361654 1034 "5" 13194139535694 true] [17592186361654 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 1319413953569